  "query_preview": "/FRIDAY THROUGH WEDNESDAY/...\nIssued at 210 PM CDT Wed Aug 26 2026\n\nFriday and Saturday, upper ridge over the Central CONUS begins to \nbreak down and shift easterly. Surface high pressure will start to \nmove east of the area on Friday, resulting in increasing llvl \nsouthwesterly flow going into Saturday. Friday will remain dry and \npleasant, with temperatures in the 80s, lower humidity, and mostly \nclear skies. With return flow kicking in Saturday, we will start to \nsee temperatures and moisture gradually increase through the day. \nPrecipitation chances have been trending upwards for Saturday, as a \nwave slowly approaches Friday night and meanders through the area on \nSaturday. Some guidance would indicate the potential for a decaying \nline of showers/storms in the morning, with the potential for \nredevelopment along any outflow that afternoon. Although, much \nuncertainty exists owing to plenty of dry air for this to battle.\nThis will be something that we keep an eye on over the coming days. \nWill stick with the NBM PoPs at this point, where it highlights \nareas largely north of Interstate 80 in 20-45% chances. \n\nSaturday night and beyond, better llvl return flow will be centered \nover the area, resulting in increasing temperatures and moisture. \nTemperatures in the upper 80s to low 90s will be possible, \nespecially in our south. With increasing humidity as well, some \nareas may have heat indices approaching 100. The strengthening LLJ \nwill allow PoPs to continue overnight Saturday into Sunday, largely \nnorth of Interstate 80. Upper level flow will generally be zonal to \nsouthwesterly through the remainder of the forecast period, which \nmay put us under the active storm path. Thus, if this pattern holds \ntrue, we could be heading into a potentially more active \nstretch of weather into next week. Global models are in general \nagreement on chances for precipitation, with some differences in\nthe timing and track of these shortwaves. Although, the NBM  \nintroduces daily PoPs (20-40%) Sunday into the middle of next \nweek. Too soon to determine any severe threat.",
